WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.118
Counting problems ask for answers modulo a large prime such as one billion and seven to prevent huge number arithmetic.

00:00:08.118 --> 00:00:16.441
Apply the modulo operator after every addition, subtraction, and multiplication so integers stay within machine word size.

00:00:16.441 --> 00:00:22.240
Python's modulo always returns a non-negative remainder when the modulus is positive.

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:29.692
Binary exponentiation raises a base to an exponent modulo MOD in O(log exp) multiplications.

00:00:29.692 --> 00:00:38.681
Always pass the third modulus argument to Python's built-in pow function instead of reducing after a massive power.

00:00:38.681 --> 00:00:44.700
Division modulo a prime p multiplies by the modular inverse pow(b, p - 2, p).

00:00:44.958 --> 00:00:52.746
To answer many n-choose-r queries in constant time, precompute factorials up to N in a forward loop.

00:00:52.746 --> 00:01:03.338
Compute a single modular inverse for fact[N] at the top, then multiply downward by i to fill all inverse factorials in linear O(N) time.

00:01:03.338 --> 00:01:08.478
Every combination nCr then takes just two modular multiplications.

00:01:08.750 --> 00:01:16.647
Stars and Bars counts ways to distribute n identical items into k distinct bins using C(n + k - 1, k - 1).

00:01:16.647 --> 00:01:25.587
When counting items that avoid several forbidden properties, use Inclusion-Exclusion over all subsets of violated rules.

00:01:25.587 --> 00:01:30.281
Alternating signs cancel out overcounted intersections cleanly.
